        "timestamp": 1780392968
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe I was a bit too verbose but I wanted to put in helpful details like the PRs and the line numbers. Are you saying that is a sign of an LLM? Did my response not make sense?</p>",
        "id": 599356729,
        "sender_full_name": "Dominic Steinitz",
        "timestamp": 1780393158
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Many things about it made me think it was LLM output, so I just wanted to ask, apologies if I've offended you</p>",
        "id": 599358435,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1780393660
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"933054\">@Snir Broshi</span> there are many lacunae in differential geometry in Mathlib and this is but one of them with the most egregious being differential forms. Lee 9.12 which relies on my <strong>proof wanted</strong> is what I proved. BTW Lee has a proof on the smoothness of the exponential Lie map which I think is not actually a proof; at least I wasn't able to formalise it and ended up using Meinrencken's which uses Lee 9.12 (which I did manage to formalise).</p>",
        "id": 599359925,
        "sender_full_name": "Dominic Steinitz",
        "timestamp": 1780394047
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And no I did not use a thesaurus - the word lacuna has been known to me since I read Dorothy Sayers many decades ago.</p>",
        "id": 599361073,
        "sender_full_name": "Dominic Steinitz",
        "timestamp": 1780394395
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I’ve never seen an LLM generate the word lacuna(e) before so I believe you<br>\n<span aria-label=\"100\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4af\" role=\"img\" title=\"100\">:100:</span> <span aria-label=\"smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f604\" role=\"img\" title=\"smile\">:smile:</span></p>\n<p>Thanks for the context!</p>",
        "id": 599554397,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Korbonits",
        "timestamp": 1780416756
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>OK I'm doing a follow-up PR now! <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41661\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41661</a> LMK if you think these lemmas are helpful.</p>",
